     iippsseeccccttll — control flows for IPsec

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     iippsseeccccttll [--ccddFFkkmmnnvv] [--DD _m_a_c_r_o=_v_a_l_u_e] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--ii _f_i_f_o] [--ss _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The iippsseeccccttll utility controls flows that determine which packets are to
     be processed by IPsec.  It allows ruleset configuration, and retrieval of
     status information from the kernel's SPD (Security Policy Database) and
     SAD (Security Association Database).  It also can control isakmpd(8) and
     establish tunnels using automatic keying with isakmpd(8).  The ruleset
     grammar is described in ipsec.conf(5).

     The options are as follows:

     --cc      Use in combination with the --ss option to collapse flow output.

     --DD _m_a_c_r_o=_v_a_l_u_e
             Define _m_a_c_r_o to be set to _v_a_l_u_e on the command line.  Overrides
             the definition of _m_a_c_r_o in the ruleset.

     --dd      When the --dd option is set, specified flows will be deleted from
             the SPD.  Otherwise, iippsseeccccttll will add flows.

     --FF      The --FF option flushes the SPD and the SAD.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Load the rules contained in _f_i_l_e.

     --ii _f_i_f_o
             If given, the --ii option specifies an alternate FIFO instead of
             _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_i_s_a_k_m_p_d_._f_i_f_o, used to talk to isakmpd(8).

     --kk      Show secret keying material when printing the active SAD entries.

     --mm      Continuously display all PF_KEY messages exchanged with the ker‐
             nel.

     --nn      Do not actually load rules, just parse them.

     --ss _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r
             Show the kernel's databases, specified by _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r (may be abbre‐
             viated):

             --ss ffllooww        Show the ruleset loaded into the SPD.
             --ss ssaa          Show the active SAD entries.
             --ss aallll         Show all of the above.

     --vv      Produce more verbose output.  A second use of --vv will produce
             even more verbose output.

